00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.930 align:start position:0%
a taste for the classics is on the rise  inspiring droves of nostalgic gamers to  seek out old games and consoles but not  everyone wants to wade through flea  markets or enter into a bidding war in  eBay for 20 year old Super Nintendo this  is where a hardware company like hyper  Ken comes into play its line of retron  consoles has given fans of classic games  a new way to play Nintendo's Super  Nintendo and Sega Genesis cartridges and  its latest console the retron 5  introduces support for famicom Game Boy  Game Boy Color and Game Boy Advance  games everything is powered by an  embedded Android operating system which  opens the door for valuable features  such as HDMI output savestates and game  genie like cheat codes it's a great  system with loads of promise but it also  has a few issues that need to be ironed  out if hyper Kin wants to please  enthusiasts luckily the retron 5 has an  upgradeable operating system so these  issues may be resolved down the road but  as it is today is hyper Ken's latest  console worth $140 asking price let's  take a look Brett round 5 is a good  looking if unusual console it has 5  cartridge slots 6 controller ports and a  dock in the back for the included  wireless controller like a lot of  aftermarket consoles it's outfitted with  cheap feeling plastic but given its  attractive design its natural to forget  what it's made from and focus on how it  looks  instead the included wireless controller  isn't as good looking or well-made as  the console but as a functional piece of  hardware it's fairly sufficient it  communicates with the retron 5 via  bluetooth and has a battery that lasts  up to 8 hours it would be great if the  battery could recharge passively while  the controller was docked in the back of  the console but in reality you have to  manually connect it to the system using  a rather unusual micro USB to mini-usb  cable thankfully the 10-foot cable  offers plenty of slack so you can play  games while the controller is charging  though it has a few nice features the  controller doesn't quite measure up to  the quality of the original NES Super  Nintendo or Genesis controllers it's  convex buttons are made of hard plastic  and emit an unpleasant hollow clicky  sound instead of implementing a  directional pad Piper can opted to  install an eight-way thumb stick similar  to what you might find on a Neo Geo  Pocket it works well for some games but  it can be a death sentence from playing  difficult games with strict timing and  movement requirements it's also  unfortunate that the system can't be  turned on with the wireless controller  you have to hold down the power button  on the front of the system for five  seconds it's a better controller than  those hyper kins shipped with past  retron systems but not by much  on either side of the system are three  ports one each for NES SNES and Genesis  controllers the retirada 5 will let you  use any controller that's plugged into  the system regardless of the type of  game you're playing so you don't need to  unplug your Genesis controller when you  want to play an nes game the controls  for different systems remap  automatically to other controllers but  you can also remap button layouts  manually within the retron 5sos  unfortunately Game Boy Game Boy Color  and Game Boy Advance games remain  single-player experiences traditionally  you'd have to link to Game Boys together  by a link cable and the retron isn't  currently capable of emulating that  arrangement thankfully it does simulate  multi taps so for player games for every  other console are easily arranged  regardless of the mix of controllers  you've connected to the system  a quick look at the back of the retron  reveals an SD card slot that's used for  cheat codes screenshots firmware updates  and save state files there's also a mini  USB port for recharging controllers an  HDMI port in the AC adapter port hi  broken has thoughtfully included outlet  adapters for Europe United Kingdom  Australia and China most aftermarket  retro consoles use problematic emulation  methods to do the heavy lifting which  doesn't always work with games that use  special chips and circuitry the retron 5  is no more pure in the sense that it  still uses emulation to get the job done  though hyper can confidently asserts at  the retron 5 will ultimately support  every officially licensed game for the  consoles it supports we found a few  exceptions during our tests including  some games that work perfectly on  previous retron consoles this includes  Street Fighter Alpha 2 in the Super  Nintendo and the combination of Sonic  the Hedgehog 2 or 3 with Sonic and  Knuckles for the Sega Genesis unlicensed  games such as pure solar on the Sega  Genesis also failed to make it past the  intro screen again these may just be  temporary issues that a hyper kin can  fix with a future firmware update  unfortunately though the retron supports  multiple systems for the moment it won't  operate if you put in more than one  cartridge at a time with a retron 3 for  example you could keep a card in every  slot and manually select which one you  wanted to play so considering that this  is an unfortunate oversight for the  retron 5 but hyper ken has said that  it's working on an OS setting that would  allow you to do the same thing but for  the moment the system will report an  error if you insert more than one game  if you're used to playing classic games  through composite cables you'll be  impressed by how crisp and colorful  games look with the retron 5 and an HDTV  and because it uses HDMI you don't have  to worry about pal and NTSC video format  incompatibility  those  people will appreciate the sharp hi-def  pixels coming out of the restaurant you  do have the option of smoothing them out  with a few distinct image filters now  one filter works well for every game but  it's a great option to have and you may  find that some games look surprisingly  fresh when the right filter is applied  built-in cheat codes are another  valuable feature in hyper kins  constructing a large database of codes  that it will distribute on its website  but you'll need to supply your own SD  card in order to use them our tests with  an early version of this database were  mostly positive but there's still room  for improvement in the cheat section of  the UI currently there's no way to  automatically sort codes or to activate  and deactivate codes on mass so it can  be a bit of a chore when you want to  find one code out of a list of a few  dozen unfortunately there's no way to  enter new codes by the OS either and  there's no easy way to edit the cheat  database on a PC without manipulating  massive and unintuitive XML files  threats round 5 emulates games after  dumping them to local memory which  includes any saves that are present on  the cartridge as such any new in game  saves you created are also saved locally  but you can also upload them back to the  original cartridge this worked in almost  every case with the exception of Super  Mario RPG but we also had an issue with  the copy of The Legend of Zelda a Link  to the Past in this case the retron  looked as though it was rewriting save  files to the cartridge but it would  consistently fail to rewrite one save in  particular which went mysteriously blank  this resulted in an empty slot in a save  file that remains in limbo on the retron  5 given that you're working with  decades-old games with equally old  batteries there's always the inherent  risk that this feature will be  unreliable but this is never explicitly  spelled out in the OS itself apart from  playing thousands of games perhaps the  most useful feature of the retron 5 is  its ability to use safe States these  allow you to save at any point during  the game and restart right where you  left off it's a convenient feature that  makes getting through some of the more  difficult games a bit easier and given  that a lot of old cartridges have dead  batteries inside safe States can breathe  new life into a cartridge that's no  longer able to store its own saved games  save States cheats and battery backups  are tied to specific games which are  identified by cross-referencing the data  on a cartridge against a database of  known games in the retros local memory  which is supposed to account for the  entire library across all platforms  unfortunately when a game can't be  identified you lose access to cheats and  potentially save States - oddly the  retron 5 couldn't properly identify our  copy of Super Metroid for the Super  Nintendo even though it's a well known  game this might be due to a poor  connection between the cartridge and the  retron but  no alternate method for accessing cheats  and savestates when this occurs taking  all these features and issues into  account it's clear that the retron 5 is  not for everyone on the one hand it does  things with real cartridges that could  only previously be accomplished with PC  based emulators and roms unfortunately  the promise of a great system is  hampered by minor incompatibility issues  and half-baked features the dependence  on cart verification is too strict and  the fact that you can only have one  cartridge in at a time is disappointing  speaking as an avid collector and retro  gaming enthusiast I'm impressed with the  latest retron but given its current  limitations it's not yet a full-time  replacement for my original consoles  still it breathes new life into classic  games and despite its current issues  there are enough arguments to justify  adding it to your repertoire of classic  gaming hardware it's currently the most  expensive console of its type  but if hybrid can fix is the issues with  the OS $140.00 will be a small price to  pay for the new gold standard in retro  gaming consoles
 

